p.src:
The direct check is $\begin{aligned}\sigma_y\frac{1}{\sqrt2}
\begin{pmatrix}
1\\
i
\end{pmatrix}
&=
\frac{1}{\sqrt2}
\begin{pmatrix}
-i\,i\\
i
\end{pmatrix}
=
\frac{1}{\sqrt2}
\begin{pmatrix}
1\\
i
\end{pmatrix},\\
\sigma_y\frac{1}{\sqrt2}
\begin{pmatrix}
1\\
-i
\end{pmatrix}
&=
\frac{1}{\sqrt2}
\begin{pmatrix}
(-i)(-i)\\
i
\end{pmatrix}
=
-\frac{1}{\sqrt2}
\begin{pmatrix}
1\\
-i
\end{pmatrix}.\end{aligned}$ Thus $+i$ belongs to eigenvalue $+1$, and $-i$ belongs to eigenvalue $-1$. (The spoken assignment remains unsettled after several reversals; the signs shown here are fixed by the displayed matrix multiplication.)  Every spin-one-half state, including every state polarized along an arbitrary direction, is a linear combination of $|+z\rangle$ and $|-z\rangle$.

